Z||000000||doughboySE||02-19-2000||06:07 PM||||there its working now<BR>anyway what does everyone use for car wax?<BR>here is what i use . i use MOTHERS liquid wax when i really wax the car and MOTHERS spray wax before a car show or a quick wash and clean up.<BR>i also use something called BLACK CHROME for the spoiler / mirrors / and the ground effect as well as any other fadded black areas<BR>IF YOU GUYS USE ANYTHING DIFFERENT LET ME KNOW ESPECIALLY THE BLACK CHROME CAUSE I THINK CANADIAN TIRE HAS DISCONTINUED IT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> AND MY BLACK NEEDS IT VERY BAD AND LOOKS GREAT AFETR ITS USED.<BR>i got a simonize big ass 10 inch buffer for xmas this year so im sure my baby will look even better this year but first ill use it on my winter beater then the wifes car then the neighbours/strangers anyone till im sure i know what im doing so i dont ruin my baby <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carolina 88GT||02-19-2000||06:13 PM||||I use a spray on Turtle wax followed by some Meguiars Quick Detail.  I use some kind of formulation that Turtle came out with for the tires....seems to work pretty good.  I will post some pictures as soon as I find that friggin camera software.........If this was a Delorean forum, we would just be using those SOS pads or fine grit sandpaper||||reg||1
Z||000002||Lance||02-19-2000||06:17 PM||||I use Armorall wax. It seems to do a good job. But I don't use armorall for the dash, It heard it dryies it out and you'll get cracks. I use baby oil on the dash.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||02-19-2000||07:09 PM||||I was using Turtle wax "emerald series" and I won't be any more after finding small places by trim/etc. that I missed and left a white haxe and/or brittle chuncks. Maybe all waxes do this if you miss a spot, but Cobra uses a wax that goes on like a spray and doesn't leave any  "white hazed" spots. Cobra, if you read this post what is the product you use again!||||reg||2
Z||000004||fiero56||02-19-2000||07:45 PM||||I use Meguiars liquid polish(when the scratches are pretty bad),Carnauba paste wax as a finisher. Looks great afterwards. I use Armor All inside, and Mother Mag and Aluminum polish for the 15" wheel center caps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||wkayl||02-19-2000||07:49 PM||||As Seen On TV: DUPLI-COLOR COLOR WAX<BR>Nourishes color as it shines.<BR>Cleans,seals & shines.<BR>Fills in swirl marks and minor scratches.<BR>Safe for all finishes.<BR>No white residue or dust.<BR>Blends in with ALL shades of RED<P>Any way that is what it says on the bottle.<BR>It works pretty good but you need to wear rubber gloves when you use it or your hands will match your car for 3 days.||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||02-19-2000||07:54 PM||||I use Meguire's Cleaner/Wax liquid. It does a very good job of getting rid of road tar,sap, bird doings, light oxidation that a good wash doesn't remove.  The Meguire's clay bar system is relly great for taking off heavier oxidation, and Meguire's Quick Detail spray for showtime cleanup. Sensing a trend here? I love Meguire's stuff.<BR>One pointer for that nasty buildup... more wax does not equal more shine, just more mess. I use the thinnest layers and smallest area at a time. No more than 2 square feet at a time.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||02-19-2000||09:27 PM||||Since we are talking detailing here I have a question for all you chemical experts out there. Was talking with the neighbor last summer and he was telling me about a trick someone told him to do to strip all old wax and residue off the paint before applying wax. He said to use 1 cup of kerosene to a gallon of water and wash the car as usuall. After watching him do the trick on his MINT Porsche, it looked awsome. I am a little hesitant to do it because of plastic body panels, and possible adverse affects to my paint, etc. Is this safe to do with my Fiero? It would be nice to get all the old wax and crap off before giving a nice wax job.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carolina 88GT||02-19-2000||09:33 PM||||Hmmmmm.....not sure about using Kerosene on plastic...I know in my younger years I made tha mistake of pluggin my fuel line with a bic ink pen (changing the fuel filter)  Took me damn near 10 minutes to get the pen back outta the line and when I finally got it out, the pen finished its process of MELTING.  Course my car didnt exactly run on kerosene either so.   I know that Dawn dishwashing liquid will do a so so job.||||reg||1
Z||000009||superXmotorsports||02-19-2000||09:34 PM||||that Kerosene thing scares me!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I use Eagle One products religously.  My Vette was Polo Green and I've had a lot of black cars and the Eagle One Wet Look does a great job of filling light scratches and swirls and has a great shine.  I also endorse their leather cleaner and conditioner.  SXM.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carolina 88GT||02-19-2000||09:39 PM||||Course I have yet to convince my wife to roll nekkid in baby oil all over my car.  Not sure what it would do for the paint, but would do wonders for me.||||reg||12
Z||000011||cobra427||02-19-2000||09:42 PM||||With my car, I first wash it,dry it and then I use Armor All exterior spray.It is a clear spray. Spray it on,wipe it off and presto.A real smooth even shine. I would not recommend it on any finish that is poor but on a good finish it does a nice job.Take a look on batboys site ,page one at the bottom.<BR>Meguiar's makes a similar product called Clear Coat Paint protection. Its about the same thing.<BR>Mine is not a daily driver and is garaged most of the time, so I can keep it lookng pretty practically all year round||||reg||1
Z||000012||doughboySE||02-19-2000||09:56 PM||||COBRA how abouts a pic of your baby for us?<BR>also i didnt see what anyone uses on there ground effects or there black trim or am i the only one with faded parts?||||reg||1
Z||000013||superXmotorsports||02-19-2000||10:10 PM||||Armor all works wonders on those faded factory-black ground effects.  My 1st Fiero I had with those I kept waxing and cleaning and couldn't figure out why they were still dull.  Spray it on heavy and let it soak in (don't even bother to wipe it off) a couple different times and it should return to a black finish instead of grey!  SXM.||||reg||1
Z||000014||wkayl||02-19-2000||10:17 PM||||After you have washed off your engine and arn't going to drive it anymore that day, spray entire engine compartment with Son-Of-A Gun, shut the lid and come back in the morning. You don't even have to wipe it off.  Works for the front area where radiator and headlights are also.||||reg||1
